The function under test is open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. Its documented rule says: “Every top-level balanced object in the text is a candidate, not only the span from the first ``{`` to the last ``}``: reasoning prose before or after the answer frequently contains stray braces, and an object nested inside another is not a candidate of its own.” I read this as requiring that an object appearing inside a string is not a top-level balanced object and therefore must not be recovered.

The two reply texts are as follows. Reply A is the exact 14-character string consisting of a double quote, the letters a, b, c, a space, a left brace, a double quote, the letter a, a double quote, a colon, a space, the digit 1, a right brace, and a double quote — that is, `"abc {"a": 1}"`. In this text the left brace is inside a string that begins with the first double quote, so the object `{"a": 1}` is not top-level. The rule therefore requires recover_json_object to find no candidate and raise RecordError. Reply B is the exact string `{"a": 1}`. Here the object is top-level, so the rule requires recover_json_object to return the dict `{"a": 1}` with no duplicate keys.

The check gives the same answer for both replies: it returns the dict `{"a": 1}` (and an empty duplicates tuple) for each, because its top-level scanner sees the left brace in Reply A and raw-decodes `{"a": 1}` from that position, ignoring that the brace is inside a string. What would make me wrong is the observation that recover_json_object on Reply A raises RecordError or returns something other than the dict `{"a": 1}`; in that case the two answers would not be the same and the conjecture would be false.